{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>You shall not take vengeance nor bear a grudge against the children of your people.</b> It does not say 'your fellow,' because vengeance and grudge-bearing are an evil trait regarding a person's body and property, and it is not fitting to seek revenge on account of a person who is among the children of your people, whether he be good or bad — for presumably the revenge is for what he did to you in your body or in your property, and those things are not so precious and significant that they would warrant taking revenge over them. However, it is permitted to take revenge against the nations, for presumably they wish to drive you away from the commandments of HaShem and to turn you aside from HaShem your Elohim, and this is a significant and precious matter that warrants seeking revenge, for it is called the vengeance of HaShem. Therefore it says here: 'against the children of your people,' to exclude the nations who are not of the children of your people, as it says (Psalm 149:7): 'To take vengeance upon the nations,' for it is the vengeance of HaShem. And concerning this our Rabbis, blessed be their memory, said (Berakhot 33a): Great is vengeance, for it is placed between two [divine] names, as it says: 'אֵל נְקָמוֹת ה׳' (ibid. 94:1). They pointed with a finger that the beginning and end of vengeance should be only for the sake of HaShem — that is, in a case where some person seeks to turn you away from the commandments of HaShem, for great is one who causes a person to sin more than one who kills him, as Rashi explained on the verse 'You shall not abominate an Egyptian' (Deuteronomy 23:8). And our Rabbis, blessed be their memory, said that any scholar who does not take vengeance and bear a grudge like a snake is not a scholar (Yoma 22b) — and this is for the honor of the Torah of HaShem that is within him." "But regarding all occurrences that befall the body, HaShem did not desire that a person seek revenge for them. A parable — to what may the matter be compared? To a toddler playing and making childish efforts with some building or something else, and some person comes and ruins all the works that the child built and planted. The child cries out before his father with a great and bitter cry over this, and if his father were to pay attention to him and do his will he would kill this man. But his father pays no attention to his voice at all, for even though the little one, according to the paucity of his intellect, thinks that this person has done him a great evil, the father nonetheless sees with his intellect that the matter is not so — and that all the child's works were vanities, delusions, and not worth being made an enemy and taking vengeance over them, for in truth this person did nothing to him at all. Such is the matter between our Father in heaven and His creatures: according to the meagreness of their grasp of intelligibles, they think that all the affairs of this world have some aspect of worth and perfection, and whoever touches their honor or their body or their property appears to them to have done them a great evil, and they cry out to their Father in heaven to avenge their cause. But sometimes the Holy One, blessed be He, does not heed the sound of their outcry when there is in this evil no guarantee of a matter touching a person's soul, for in the eyes of HaShem, blessed be He, all the affairs that people contrive for themselves in this world are exactly like the laughter and play of the aforementioned child. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, does not attend to them except in a case where, through the events that befall the body, nullification of Torah and commandment will result — then the vengeance is the vengeance of HaShem." "And upon this matter the psalm beginning 'אֵל נְקָמוֹת ה׳' etc. (Psalm 94:1) is founded, which prefaces by saying that one should not seek vengeance except in a matter that is the vengeance of HaShem as stated. Afterward it says, 'How long shall the wicked exult, HaShem?' etc., up to 'HaShem knows the thoughts of man, that they are vanity.' And the explanation of the matter is that it comes to quiet the complaints of those who say that HaShem has forsaken the earth, and who declare that surely 'Yah will not see, and will not take notice of all the doings of the lowly' — for how could it be that He who sees and understands allows the destruction of His birthright — the poor and the crushed, the widow, the stranger, and the orphan — and does not take vengeance upon the evildoers? This is only from the perspective that His supervision, blessed be He, does not extend to the lowly. Concerning this he says, as if responding, that the matter is not so — but rather because 'HaShem knows the thoughts of man, that they are vanity,' like the parable of the playing toddler; therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, is not exacting with them at all times. And upon this the entire psalm that says 'How long shall the wicked exult, HaShem?' etc. is founded. After he said 'אֵל נְקָמוֹת ה׳,' he took up the argument against those who say 'He will not see, Yah' etc. — as Scripture says: 'Your people, HaShem, they crush, and Your heritage they oppress, and they say Yah will not see' etc. For if not so, why does He not take vengeance upon them? Concerning this he answered and said: 'Consider, you brutish among the people' etc. 'He who planted the ear, shall He not hear? He who formed the eye, shall He not see?' etc. And if you say: after He hears and sees, why does He not take vengeance upon them, since He is 'אֵל נְקָמוֹת ה׳'? Concerning this he says, as if responding: 'HaShem knows the thoughts of man, that they are vanity' — for if the events that befall the body are in our eyes a great matter, in the eyes of HaShem they are discipline of vanities (Jeremiah 10:8), like the parable of the playing toddler. And if you say: in any case, why should HaShem see toil and anger when the wicked lays siege to the righteous? Concerning this he says: 'Happy is the man whom You discipline, Yah, and whom You teach from Your Torah' — for him whom HaShem desires, him He rebukes through suffering, provided there is no nullification of Torah in them, as our Rabbis, blessed be their memory, learned from the verse 'and from Your Torah You teach him' (Berakhot 5a) — for if there were nullification of Torah in them, the Holy One, blessed be He, would take vengeance upon those who cause its nullification. Therefore it says 'You shall not take vengeance' etc., for all the affairs of this world are not significant before HaShem, blessed be He, and it is as though he did nothing at all to him; therefore it is not fitting to seek revenge on account of any person who is of His people." "<b>And you shall love your fellow as yourself.</b> Our Rabbis, blessed be their memory, said: this is a great general principle in the Torah. And in tractate Shabbat (31a) there was an incident with a certain convert who said: 'Teach me the entire Torah while I stand on one foot.' And Hillel taught him the verse 'And you shall love your fellow as yourself' — 'What is hateful to you, do not do to your fellow; that is the entire Torah, the rest is commentary — go and learn.' Now according to what appears, the convert was a righteous convert and was not one of the scoffers who speak in jest to say, by way of mockery, that they should teach him the entire Torah while he literally stands on one foot. Rather, he surely genuinely sought that they establish for him all the commandments of the Torah upon one foundation — that is, one 'leg' upon which he would set all the commandments — so that he would not come to the forgetfulness common in a convert who learned nothing from his youth of the commandments of the Torah. Therefore he asked them to give him one general principle that encompasses the entire Torah, and by way of metaphor he said 'while I stand on one foot,' and his intention was to receive from him something stated quickly in concise language — and that is also a foundation and one 'leg,' and through remembering this principle he would remember all the commandments of HaShem." "And Hillel taught him the verse 'And you shall love your fellow as yourself — I am HaShem,' for our Rabbis, blessed be their memory, already said (Makkot 24a): Habakkuk came and established them upon one, as it says: 'But the righteous shall live by his faith' (Habakkuk 2:4). And this does not contradict the words of Hillel, for all the commandments of the Torah are of two types: the first is the commandments between a person and the Omnipresent, and the foundation for all of them is faith in HaShem; the second is the commandments between a person and his fellow, and the foundation for all of them is the verse 'And you shall love your fellow as yourself.' And for this reason the cherubim spread their wings upward, pointing toward above, corresponding to the commandments between a person and the Omnipresent, and their faces were each toward the other, corresponding to the commandments between a person and his fellow. And when he said here 'on one leg,' it means one foundation for each type. Therefore he told him the verse 'And you shall love your fellow as yourself,' and upon this 'leg' he established for him all the commandments between a person and his fellow, and he also told him the end of the verse: 'I am HaShem' — that is, the foundation upon which Habakkuk established all the commandments of the Torah, namely faith in HaShem. And the statement 'I am HaShem' parallels the utterance 'I am,' which encompasses all the first utterances between a person and the Omnipresent, and the seal of them all is honoring father and mother, for from the end of his matter it is recognized that the beginning of his matter is true, in that an a fortiori argument may be drawn from honoring father and mother to honoring the third Partner, blessed be His name — 'Is He not your Father who acquired you?' And the utterance 'You shall not covet' encompasses all five latter utterances, and it corresponds to 'And you shall love your fellow as yourself,' for both of them encompass all the commandments between a person and his fellow. And so Rabbi Levi said (Vayikra Rabbah 24:5): Ten utterances are written in this section, and he concludes that the utterance 'You shall not covet' corresponds to 'And you shall love your fellow as yourself' — see there."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "The phrase וְלֹא יָבִין בְּכָל מַעֲשֵׂה הַשְּׁפָלִים ('and will not take notice of all the doings of the lowly') echoes Psalm 94:7; translated as scriptural echo."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:00.920599807Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}